1
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:09,164
Radar satellites can see through clouds and darkness, but their images come

2
00:00:09,164 --> 00:00:10,487
with a catch: speckle.

3
00:00:10,538 --> 00:00:14,992
Every synthetic aperture radar picture is covered in grainy noise, so even

4
00:00:14,992 --> 00:00:17,400
finding a vehicle on the ground is hard.

5
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:22,424
And the classical methods that clean this up get slow, or stuck, as images

6
00:00:22,424 --> 00:00:22,760
grow.

7
00:00:24,056 --> 00:00:27,481
QuantumSAR turns segmentation into an energy puzzle.

8
00:00:27,531 --> 00:00:31,595
Each pixel gets a label, target or background, which we write as a tiny

9
00:00:31,595 --> 00:00:33,656
magnet, a spin, pointing up or down.

10
00:00:33,706 --> 00:00:38,194
Neighbouring spins like to agree, and bright pixels prefer to be target.

11
00:00:38,244 --> 00:00:42,941
That is an Ising problem, exactly the kind of optimisation quantum computers

12
00:00:42,941 --> 00:00:43,806
are built for.

13
00:00:43,856 --> 00:00:49,149
We solve it with QAOA, the Quantum Approximate Optimisation Algorithm, running

14
00:00:49,149 --> 00:00:49,896
on Classiq.

15
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:52,060
Let's try it.

16
00:00:52,110 --> 00:00:55,660
I pick a noisy radar sample and choose the quantum solver.

17
00:00:55,710 --> 00:01:00,504
A sixty-four by sixty-four image has four thousand pixels, far more than any

18
00:01:00,504 --> 00:01:05,047
simulator has qubits, so the app cuts it into tiny three-by-three tiles.

19
00:01:05,097 --> 00:01:09,848
Each tile becomes a nine-qubit circuit, and Classiq runs a whole batch of

20
00:01:09,848 --> 00:01:10,760
tiles at once.

21
00:01:10,810 --> 00:01:15,301
The badge always says where a result came from: this one was computed on

22
00:01:15,301 --> 00:01:17,735
Classiq's cloud simulator, then cached.

23
00:01:17,785 --> 00:01:21,660
Orange is the target, and it matches the ground truth closely.

24
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:23,434
Now switch on three classes.

25
00:01:23,484 --> 00:01:28,210
Every pixel now uses three qubits, and the dark radar shadow behind the

26
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:29,807
vehicle appears in blue.

27
00:01:31,134 --> 00:01:36,096
Turn on edge detection, and the app outlines every region, one pixel wide.

28
00:01:36,146 --> 00:01:41,642
Red traces the vehicle, cyan traces its shadow, ready for a target-recognition

29
00:01:41,642 --> 00:01:41,994
step.

30
00:01:43,337 --> 00:01:44,562
So how does it compare?

31
00:01:44,612 --> 00:01:47,837
One click benchmarks every solver on the same image.

32
00:01:47,887 --> 00:01:52,794
Quantum QAOA lands within a fraction of a percent of the exact optimum found

33
00:01:52,794 --> 00:01:57,637
by graph cut, and far ahead of Otsu and k-means thresholding, which speckle

34
00:01:57,637 --> 00:01:58,024
fools.

35
00:01:58,074 --> 00:02:02,300
To be fair, annealing and graph cut are still faster on a laptop.

36
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:04,617
And everything exports to CSV.

37
00:02:05,931 --> 00:02:07,606
Now, the honest limits.

38
00:02:07,656 --> 00:02:12,684
Qubit counts keep tiles small, so the quantum part optimises local blocks, not

39
00:02:12,684 --> 00:02:14,231
the whole image at once.

40
00:02:14,281 --> 00:02:18,835
Live cloud runs take a few minutes per image, which is why this demo serves

41
00:02:18,835 --> 00:02:20,231
cached Classiq results.

42
00:02:20,281 --> 00:02:25,173
Bigger devices mean bigger tiles, and next we want real MSTAR data and

43
00:02:25,173 --> 00:02:26,151
hardware runs.

44
00:02:28,062 --> 00:02:32,650
QuantumSAR is open source, tested, and runs with one Docker command.

45
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,262
Grab the code and try the live demo from the links on screen.
